(4814354, 694402, 51538, '<td valign="top">Another record: no comments received during voting.  And almost a 6.  And no 1s.  So mainstream.  The enigma is disappearing, Don.</td>', 'Another record: no comments received during voting.  And almost a 6.  And no 1s.  So mainstream.  The enigma is disappearing, Don.', '2008-07-09 10:26:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(4814362, 694208, 67152, '<td valign="top">Jon<br/>The layers created by the grass, the trees, and the sky are well placed and give this the prototypical rule of thirds feel.  The church looks like it has a lot of charm and interest, but with the small image size (don''t you just hate 640?) the details are mostly lost<br/> <br/>I just wonder about the choice of b/w here - were you trying to have the sky and the field look similar as if it is a reflection?  If so, I think more details in the grass field are needed to give it the shimmering feel.  If not the point, I think a color version may have been a better approach - to highlight the warm summer sky and the glow off the grass.<br/> <br/>I really think the main reason for the low-ish score is an overall softness to the image...don''t forget that us DPCers need it sharp, sharp, sharp!</td>', 'JonThe layers created by the grass, the trees, and the sky are well placed and give this the prototypical rule of thirds feel.  The church looks like it has a lot of charm and interest, but with the small image size (don''t you just hate 640?) the details are mostly lost I just wonder about the choice of b/w here - were you trying to have the sky and the field look similar as if it is a reflection?  If so, I think more details in the grass field are needed to give it the shimmering feel.  If not the point, I think a color version may have been a better approach - to highlight the warm summer sky and the glow off the grass. I really think the main reason for the low-ish score is an overall softness to the image...don''t forget that us DPCers need it sharp, sharp, sharp!', '2008-07-09 10:31:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(4814367, 694378, 79720, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by echoman:</b></div><hr/><i> is that sky natural? looks like you darkened parts. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Yep, it is. The RAW file was converted to DNG, and the DNG was tone-mapped in Photomatix. The "curves" adjustment in Photoshop brought out the sky. Of course, that left the foreground painfully over-staurated. :-(</td>', 'Originally posted by echoman: is that sky natural? looks like you darkened parts.   Yep, it is. The RAW file was converted to DNG, and the DNG was tone-mapped in Photomatix. The "curves" adjustment in Photoshop brought out the sky. Of course, that left the foreground painfully over-staurated. :-(', '2008-07-09 10:35:28.000000', NULL, 0),
(4814377, 694378, 67152, '<td valign="top">This shot is great and I am happy it scored so well. But, do you realize that Photomatix Pro is illegal in basic?  It is unfortunate becuase Photomatix pro does much the same as Shadows/Highlights does in PS for a single image and shadows/ highlights are allowed in basic...strange huh?<br/> <br/><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by david_c:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by echoman:</b></div><hr/><i> is that sky natural? looks like you darkened parts. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Yep, it is. The RAW file was converted to DNG, and the DNG was tone-mapped in Photomatix. The "curves" adjustment in Photoshop brought out the sky. Of course, that left the foreground painfully over-staurated. :-( </i></td></tr></table><br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2008-07-09 09:42:31.</i></td>', 'This shot is great and I am happy it scored so well. But, do you realize that Photomatix Pro is illegal in basic?  It is unfortunate becuase Photomatix pro does much the same as Shadows/Highlights does in PS for a single image and shadows/ highlights are allowed in basic...strange huh? Originally posted by david_c: Originally posted by echoman: is that sky natural? looks like you darkened parts.   Yep, it is. The RAW file was converted to DNG, and the DNG was tone-mapped in Photomatix. The "curves" adjustment in Photoshop brought out the sky. Of course, that left the foreground painfully over-staurated. :-( ', '2008-07-09 10:39:27.000000', '2008-07-09 10:42:31.000000', 0),
(4814381, 693614, 61222, '<td valign="top">Beautiful image. I am sorry I did not have time to vote. This is awesome. :-)</td>', 'Beautiful image. I am sorry I did not have time to vote. This is awesome. :-)', '2008-07-09 10:40:29.000000', NULL, 0),
